WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:01.590
CARTER MORGAN: Hi,
I'm Carter Morgan,

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.500
and today I'll be talking to
you about API-first application

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.557
development.

00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:07.140
Whether you're
adopting cloud services

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.130
or transitioning to a
cloud infrastructure,

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.520
an API-first approach can
help you manage the complexity

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.237
enveloping new applications.

00:00:13.237 --> 00:00:14.070
Let's check out how.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:24.300
As organizations
are increasingly

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.030
leveraging cloud technologies
to build applications,

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.640
they implement processes to
orchestrate new microservices

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.375
architectures, containerization,
and continuous delivery.

00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:33.750
But where do they
even start when

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.422
developing a new application?

00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:38.130
As the first step,
they need to make sure

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.255
that the data that will be
powering the application

00:00:40.255 --> 00:00:42.180
is exposed as an API.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.730
The developers responsible for
the rest of the application

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.285
will be the first
consumers of this API.

00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:48.660
This ensures that
the application

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.180
has the quality,
predictability, and stability

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.910
to withstand web clients, mobile
users, and other developer

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.530
consumers.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.750
But although APIs help
you unlock innovation,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.672
in order to accelerate
it, organizations

00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:02.130
need to rethink their workforce
to expand their application

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.587
development bandwidth.

00:01:03.587 --> 00:01:05.129
Their traditional
code-first approach

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.630
to application
development sometimes

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.150
results in delays, rework,
or disjointed experiences

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.410
for end users.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.360
Additionally there is
a large growing deficit

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.890
of developers in the world.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.540
And as a result,
IT organizations

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.330
are experiencing a huge
backlog of software requests,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.720
slow development cycles,
and security challenges.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.160
Because of this,
organizations are

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.710
adopting no-code application
development solutions along

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.745
with an API-first approach to
empower their line of business

00:01:28.745 --> 00:01:30.870
workers to build applications
within the guardrails

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.390
prescribed by IT.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.980
Why, you may ask?

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.230
Well, there are several benefits
for choosing this approach.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.620
First, by decoupling
internal independencies

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.120
from the back end
to the front end,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.990
work can progress faster
and independently.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.450
Next, by showing only
the necessary details

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.520
to the intended
users via APIs, we

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.050
hide the complexity
of connecting

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.850
to data and services.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.010
And lastly, leveraging
a no-code approach

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.920
allows companies to quickly
create high-fidelity prototypes

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.270
and gather feedback for
end users and iterate.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.210
Now, let's talk a little bit
about how you can leverage APIs

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.160
to build applications
without coding

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.020
using Apigee and AppSheet.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.270
You can easily connect
to existing Apigee APIs

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.280
within Apigee,
customize applications,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.470
and deploy to mobile web
and desktop interfaces

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.150
with the click of a button.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.940
This shortens development
timelines considerably.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.670
Let's look at a quick
demo of how this works.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.890
We're going to start in the
Apigee environment, where

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.410
we have an open API
specification, an API

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.560
proxy that represents that
spec, and an API product which

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.430
publishes the API proxy for
client applications to consume.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.630
Today, an app created in
AppSheet will be our client.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.670
In our Hipster
products API proxy,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:46.550
we can see a preflow policy
that checks for an API key.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.127
This helps to secure
the API, as only

00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:50.335
apps that have this secret
key it can invoke the API.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.090
Next, we can see
the API product that

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.460
publishes the API for
client applications

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.210
to use called Hipster Silver.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.380
At the bottom of the
API product definition,

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.470
we can see that an app has
been registered already

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.410
for AppSheet.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.690
And this is where we get
the API key for our app.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.850
Now we're going to
switch personas.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.472
We've been an Apigee API
producer up until now.

00:03:15.472 --> 00:03:16.930
In AppSheet, we
will put on the hat

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.160
of the line of business user.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.610
The first thing we'll do
is create a data source

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.310
to point to our API in Apigee.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.260
We'll click on + data
source to add a new one.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.030
We will name the data
source Apigee Hipsters Demo

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.250
and select the Apigee type.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.580
We enter the API key
from Apigee and the URL

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.120
to the OpenAPI Spec.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.120
And now we can validate
and then authorize access

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.830
to create the data source.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.590
Next, we can create an app.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.810
We will go to the My Apps area.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.090
AppSheet noticed that we
don't have any apps yet,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.980
so it's assuming we
want to create one.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.360
We do.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.610
We'll use the Start from
Your Own Data Option,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.880
and the data will come
from our Apigee API.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.270
We'll call this
app Hipsters App.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.320
Now is when we get to choose the
Apigee data source we created.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.070
I'll select Apigee
Hipsters Demo.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.960
Let's select Products.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.690
AppSheet just created
the app for us.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.732
And we can see the preview
of the app in the pane

00:04:13.732 --> 00:04:15.280
on the right already.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.662
We need to just make
a few adjustments.

00:04:17.662 --> 00:04:20.079
I know that there's a picture
of each product in the data,

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.980
but it's not showing up.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:25.770
We'll change the picture
data type to Image.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.660
The product ID is kind of ugly.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.870
Let's change that
app to not show that,

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.280
and then it will show
the product name first.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.755
Let's save our changes.

00:04:33.755 --> 00:04:35.880
Now the images are showing
up and the product names

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.000
look great.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.512
AppSheet helped us create our
first app within a minute.

00:04:40.512 --> 00:04:41.970
This is how you
can easily leverage

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.630
Apigee API to build no-code
applications with AppSheet.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.150
In summary, the Apigee
data source for AppSheet

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.730
gives you an easy way to
leverage your existing APIs

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.790
to develop no-code
applications within AppSheet.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.860
By abstracting data
and services as APIs,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.967
and using these APIs to build
applications without coding,

00:04:58.967 --> 00:05:01.050
organizations can accelerate
development timelines

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.793
considerably, and reduce
their application backlogs.

00:05:03.793 --> 00:05:05.460
Now it's your turn
to try it out and get

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.752
started by clicking the links
in the description below.

00:05:07.752 --> 00:05:10.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

